st += [p('Executive decision','H1x'),p('<b>Recommendation:</b> Adopt a local-first, two-engine data architecture in stages. Keep SQLite as the durable transactional source of truth for users, portfolios, positions, watchlists, alerts, ingestion state, and operational metadata. Introduce DuckDB as a local analytical engine for large historical scans, feature generation, research aggregates, and ML training datasets only after a representative benchmark. Treat Parquet files as reproducible analytical snapshots, not a second authority.','Calloutx'),p('This is a workload separation recommendation, not a claim that replacing SQLite will automatically make every screen faster. The current app is already local and already uses SQLite in WAL mode. The largest evidenced opportunity is the mixed workload: one 1.54 GB SQLite file holds transactional state alongside millions of market bars and high-volume option data. A columnar engine can scan and aggregate those tables efficiently, while SQLite continues to handle the small, correctness-sensitive transactions it is designed for.'),tbl(['Decision area','Recommendation'],[
('Operational truth','SQLite remains the only writer/authority for operational and user-owned records.'),('Analytics and ML','DuckDB reads curated local snapshots and computes broad scans/features; no direct portfolio writes.'),('Data files','Parquet partitions for rebuildable, versioned analytical snapshots where measured beneficial.'),('User experience','Keep Python + plain JavaScript + local app process; improve query plans, summaries, cache, and provenance before framework change.'),('Deployment','No mandatory database server or network dependency; local startup and offline access stay first-class.')],[1.35*inch,5.85*inch]),p('What this report does not assume','H2x')]
